Network Topologies
1. What is Network Topology?

Network topology describes how devices such as computers, switches, routers, and other network devices are physically or logically connected to each other.

It helps us understand how devices communicate and how the network is organized.

Types of Network Topology

The main topologies are:

Bus
Star
Ring
Mesh
Hybrid


2. Bus Topology

In a Bus topology, all devices are connected to a single main cable called the backbone.

Advantages
Simple design
Requires less cable
Relatively inexpensive
Easy to set up for small networks



Disadvantages
Backbone failure can affect the entire network
Performance decreases as devices increase
Troubleshooting can be difficult
Limited scalability
Data collisions can occur in traditional shared Ethernet



Real-World Use
Bus topology was used in older Ethernet networks, especially networks using coaxial cable.

It is rarely used in modern enterprise networks.



3. Star Topology ⭐

In a Star topology, every device connects to a central network device, usually a switch


Advantages
Easy to install and manage
Easy troubleshooting
Failure of one cable usually affects only one device
Easy to add or remove devices
Good scalability


Disadvantages
Failure of the central switch can affect connected devices
Requires more cable than Bus topology
Requires a central device


Real-World Use ⭐
Star topology is very common in modern LANs.

For example:

PC ──┐
Laptop ──┤
Printer ─┼── Switch ── Router
Server ──┤
AP ──────┘

This is one of the most important topologies to understand for IT Support, Networking, AWS Cloud, and DevOps fundamentals.

4. Ring Topology

In a Ring topology, each device is connected to two neighboring devices, forming a closed loop.


Data travels around the ring according to the network's design.

Advantages
Organized data flow
Can provide predictable access in some implementations
No central switch is required in the basic topology


Disadvantages
A link/device failure can affect communication in a traditional single-ring design
Troubleshooting can be difficult
Adding/removing devices can be more complicated
Less common in modern LANs


Real-World Use
Ring topology was historically used in technologies such as Token Ring.

Some specialized network technologies can use ring-based designs for redundancy and resilient connectivity, but traditional ring LANs are uncommon today.

5. Mesh Topology

In a Mesh topology, devices have multiple connections with other devices.

Full Mesh

Every device connects directly to every other device.

Partial Mesh

Only some devices have multiple connections.

Advantages
High redundancy
Multiple paths are available
Better fault tolerance
Failure of one link does not necessarily stop communication


Disadvantages
Expensive
Requires more cables/connections
More complex to configure and manage
Full mesh becomes difficult to scale



Real-World Use
Mesh concepts are commonly seen in:

WAN designs
Wireless mesh networks
Data-center/network redundancy
Connections between important network devices




